By the way, have you realized that these calculations could largely
simplify your task?   I guess you were thinking of generating the whole
probability distributions (i.e. how counting how many times there were
zero, one, two and so on potions of healing, extra healing, etc. in a
large sample of games ).  Because my calculations based on the number of
potions generated (without considering how many levels you went to get
that many) are exact (despite being simplistic), all you need to measure
is the average number of *healing potions per level.  Once we have that,
we can calculate everything analytically.  [The only assumption I have
to make for *this* to hold is that the random number generator is not
completely flawed, i.e. that it produces the different kinds of potion
in roughly uncorrelated manner. It need not be doing so very well, as
this is not a Monte Carlo simulation, where good RNGs are more
important. Also, as others have argued before, the actions of the player
tend to reduce any correlations that the RNG may have.]


